  "text": "We are given the following two conditions involving the complex number $\\alpha$:\n\n1. The distance from $\\alpha^3$ to $2i$ is **3 times** the distance from $\\alpha$ to $2i$:\n   $$\n   \\text{Distance}(\\alpha^3, 2i) = 3 \\cdot \\text{Distance}(\\alpha, 2i)\n   $$\n\n2. The distance from $\\alpha^6$ to $2i$ is **6 times** the distance from $\\alpha$ to $2i$:\n   $$\n   \\text{Distance}(\\alpha^6, 2i) = 6 \\cdot \\text{Distance}(\\alpha, 2i)\n   $$\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Use the distance formula in complex numbers\n\nThe distance between two points in the complex plane, say $z_1 = a + bi$ and $z_2 = c + di$, is:\n$$\n|z_1 - z_2| = \\sqrt{(a - c)^2 + (b - d)^2}\n$$\n\nSo, for the first condition:\n$$\n\\sqrt{(\\alpha^3 - 2i)^2} = 3 \\cdot \\sqrt{(\\alpha - 2i)^2}\n$$\n$$\n| \\alpha^3 - 2i | = 3 | \\alpha - 2i |\n$$\n\nSquare both sides:\n$$\n( \\alpha^3 - 2i ) \\overline{(\\alpha^3 - 2i)} = 9 ( \\alpha - 2i ) \\overline{(\\alpha - 2i)}\n$$\n$$\n| \\alpha^3 |^2 = 9 | \\alpha |^2\n$$\n\nSo:\n$$\n|\\alpha^3|^2 = 9 |\\alpha|^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad |\\alpha|^2 = 9 |\\alpha|^2 \\quad \\text{[This is always true!]} \\quad \\text{So no new information.} \\quad \\text{No constraint.}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second condition:\n$$\n|\\alpha^6|^2 = 6 |\\alpha|^2\n\\Rightarrow |\\alpha|^2 = 6 |\\alpha|^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad |\\alpha|^2 = 0\n\\Rightarrow \\alpha = 0\n$$\n\nBut $\\alpha = 0$ doesn't satisfy the first condition, which would make the distances undefined.\n\nSo, we must **ignore** the second condition, and only consider the first one.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Final conclusion\n\nWe found that the second condition leads to a contradiction unless $\\alpha = 0$, which is not valid.\n\nHence, there is **no such complex number** $\\alpha$ that satisfies both conditions.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\text{No such complex number exists}}\n$$",
